==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*fleuhaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|fliā}}, {{cog|osx|fliohan}}, {{cog|odt|flian}}, {{cog|goh|fliohan}}, {{cog|non|flýja}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|flēon}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|flēon}}

# to [[run]] [[away]], [[run]] [[from]], [[escape]]
#: {{ux|ang|Mid þē sind þā þing þe þū '''flīehst'''.|The things that you '''run from''' are with you.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA148 translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|''God ne ēht nānre wihte, for þȳ hine nān wiht ne mæġ '''flēon'''.''|God doesn't chase anything, because nothing can '''run from''' him.}}
# to [[avoid]]

====Usage notes====
* ''Fleon'' was generally used transitively, as in ''*hē flēah þone beran'' ("he fled from the bear", literally "he fled the bear"). It was not used with prepositions such as {{m|ang|fram}} or  {{m|ang|of}} (both meaning "from").

